Subject: [PATCH] glassine: shm allocator: a memfd that allows sealing, not a shm_open file

wlroots' shm allocator (the pixman renderer's swapchain) made its buffers
with shm_open on /dev/shm. glassine-epdd-desktop maps the buffers this
compositor sends it as root, and (GlassineOS/epd pull request 19) refuses
any fd it cannot seal against shrinking (F_SEAL_SHRINK): a file shrunk
under its mapping would kill the daemon with SIGBUS. A shm_open file is
born with F_SEAL_SEAL, so the pixman fallback would be refused buffer by
buffer: a blank panel.

allocate_shm_file now makes a memfd with MFD_ALLOW_SEALING (read-write, as
the allocator needs), and falls back to the shm_open file only if
memfd_create fails. Nothing in wlroots shrinks the file after it is handed
out. The other caller, the input method's keymap, is unaffected by the file
being sealable.

--- a/util/shm.c
+++ b/util/shm.c
@@ -1,3 +1,4 @@
+#define _GNU_SOURCE
 #include <errno.h>
 #include <fcntl.h>
 #include <string.h>
@@ -37,12 +38,20 @@ static int excl_shm_open(char *name) {
 }
 
 int allocate_shm_file(size_t size) {
-	char name[] = RANDNAME_PATTERN;
-	int fd = excl_shm_open(name);
+	// glassine: a memfd that allows sealing. glassine-epdd-desktop maps the
+	// buffers sway sends it and refuses a file it cannot seal against
+	// shrinking (F_SEAL_SHRINK), which a shm_open file never allows. Nothing
+	// here shrinks the file after it is handed out. Without memfd_create
+	// (ENOSYS), the shm_open file as before.
+	int fd = memfd_create("wlroots", MFD_CLOEXEC | MFD_ALLOW_SEALING);
 	if (fd < 0) {
-		return -1;
+		char name[] = RANDNAME_PATTERN;
+		fd = excl_shm_open(name);
+		if (fd < 0) {
+			return -1;
+		}
+		shm_unlink(name);
 	}
-	shm_unlink(name);
 
 	int ret;
 	do {
